# ~/.config/labwc/autostart   (Raspberry Pi OS Bookworm and later use labwc)
#
# Install it as your login user, not with sudo:
#
#   mkdir -p ~/.config/labwc
#   cp /etc/xdg/labwc/autostart ~/.config/labwc/autostart   # if the system one exists
#   nano ~/.config/labwc/autostart                          # append the lines below
#   chmod 644 ~/.config/labwc/autostart
#
# Permissions: 644 on the file, 755 on ~/.config/labwc, owned by you. labwc
# runs this through `sh`, so it does not need the execute bit.
#
# IMPORTANT: a user autostart REPLACES /etc/xdg/labwc/autostart, it does not
# add to it. On Pi OS the system file is what starts the desktop panel and
# wallpaper, so copy it first unless you want a bare kiosk screen.

# 1. Set the display mode BEFORE the browser starts, and do not background it -
#    Chromium sizes its window at launch, so a mode change still in flight is a
#    race you lose intermittently.
#
#    Touch Display 2 (natively portrait, rotated into landscape):
# wlr-randr --output DSI-1 --transform 90
#
#    A bar display whose native mode is missing from the EDID. Check the output
#    name with `wlr-randr` first; the Pi 5 has HDMI-A-1 and HDMI-A-2:
# wlr-randr --output HDMI-A-1 --custom-mode 1280x400@60

# 2. Start the kiosk browser. This one is backgrounded so labwc can carry on.
/opt/unifi-dashboard/deploy/kiosk.sh &

# Screen blanking is a separate setting, not something to script here:
#   sudo raspi-config  ->  Display Options  ->  Screen Blanking  ->  No
